WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.221 --> 00:00:03.554
(accordion)

00:00:12.583 --> 00:00:14.964
(waves crashing)

00:00:14.964 --> 00:00:16.918
- When I was kid, we used
to live in Dark Harbor

00:00:16.918 --> 00:00:18.445
during part of the summer months

00:00:18.445 --> 00:00:21.089
when we was working out there.

00:00:21.089 --> 00:00:24.421
Live in little shacks,
almost like okies, you know?

00:00:24.421 --> 00:00:25.254
(laughs)

00:00:25.254 --> 00:00:26.087
Sort of.

00:00:26.087 --> 00:00:28.391
And, uh, yeah, it was great.

00:00:28.391 --> 00:00:31.058
It was really old-fashioned, too.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:36.413
(light instrumental music)

00:00:40.578 --> 00:00:42.445
Dulsing is picking an edible

00:00:42.445 --> 00:00:44.701
seaweed off the beach at low tide.

00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:46.125
(wave rushes in)

00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:47.067
(pulls seaweed)

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:51.415
My great-great-uncles back in
the early 1900s were dulsers.

00:00:51.415 --> 00:00:53.099
And then my grandfather did, too.

00:00:53.099 --> 00:00:54.839
And then my father.

00:00:54.839 --> 00:00:55.839
And then me.

00:00:56.776 --> 00:01:00.693
It went down through the
generations, for sure.

00:01:01.892 --> 00:01:03.819
Dark Harbor's a pond, I don't know,

00:01:03.819 --> 00:01:07.149
it must be over half a mile long, I guess.

00:01:07.149 --> 00:01:09.950
And it's enclosed by a natural sea wall.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.162
There's high cliffs
surrounding most of it,

00:01:12.162 --> 00:01:14.043
except the western side.

00:01:14.043 --> 00:01:15.707
I guess that's why they
always called it Dark Harbor,

00:01:15.707 --> 00:01:17.546
‘cause it takes a while for the sun to hit

00:01:17.546 --> 00:01:19.713
down there in the, in the morning.

00:01:20.803 --> 00:01:23.406
Some fisher's tried up
there that didn't pan out.

00:01:23.406 --> 00:01:25.877
They blamed it on that pirate's curse.

00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:27.908
Pirate and his crew come ashore
and they bury their treasure

00:01:27.908 --> 00:01:29.228
up on top of the hill.

00:01:29.228 --> 00:01:31.224
The captain got them all drunk and then he

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:33.870
murdered them all so he could
have the treasure himself.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:36.631
But in the process, he got
cut too and he bled to death.

00:01:36.631 --> 00:01:39.140
So anyone that has tried
to find his treasure

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.577
has all met an untimely death since then.

00:01:41.577 --> 00:01:45.744
So there's been a curse on the
whole place since then, yeah.

00:01:46.825 --> 00:01:49.575
(waves crashing)

00:01:59.444 --> 00:02:02.750
Got into it with my dad
when I was a small child.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:06.108
I started going dulsing with him.

00:02:06.108 --> 00:02:08.965
Then, as I got old
enough, I went on my own.

00:02:08.965 --> 00:02:12.715
And I'm still doing it
for the last 46 years.

00:02:14.743 --> 00:02:17.204
And that is a long time.

00:02:17.204 --> 00:02:18.775
But some of the
older guys who can't go

00:02:18.775 --> 00:02:20.325
and they tell me, they say,

00:02:20.325 --> 00:02:22.434
“Oh, go while you can,
‘cause you'll miss it.”

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:24.425
These guys are 80 years old, you know.

00:02:24.425 --> 00:02:27.224
And they miss it, they wish they could go.

00:02:27.224 --> 00:02:31.391
I don't know, I think I can
find something else to do.

00:02:32.968 --> 00:02:36.301
(light accordion music)

00:02:45.667 --> 00:02:47.133
Nothing has changed.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.361
It’s still picked by hand,

00:02:48.361 --> 00:02:50.944
still dried by the sun outside.

00:02:51.999 --> 00:02:54.832
(seaweed pouring)

00:02:55.967 --> 00:02:57.732
They're all homemade shaking machines.

00:02:57.732 --> 00:03:01.007
Just an old washing machine motor,

00:03:01.007 --> 00:03:04.438
prongs inside that turn and
thrash your belts around,

00:03:04.438 --> 00:03:06.447
and it comes out the bottom.

00:03:06.447 --> 00:03:09.317
I guess that's pretty much what it does.

00:03:09.317 --> 00:03:12.650
(light accordion music)

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.064
I just kind of like working
alone in the peace and quiet

00:03:25.064 --> 00:03:27.341
and doing my own thing without
a bunch of people around.

00:03:27.341 --> 00:03:30.411
That's the way I prefer it.

00:03:30.411 --> 00:03:33.887
(light accordion music)

00:03:33.887 --> 00:03:35.759
The only one's that go
alone, that I know of,

00:03:35.759 --> 00:03:37.033
is me and my friend Donny Richie.

00:03:37.033 --> 00:03:40.866
I think that we're the
only two that go alone.

00:03:41.772 --> 00:03:43.442
Donnie Richie came up with
the idea of putting the

00:03:43.442 --> 00:03:46.025
bicycle wheel on the other end.

00:03:47.406 --> 00:03:50.739
(light accordion music)

00:04:11.794 --> 00:04:14.461
(bugs chirping)

00:04:28.644 --> 00:04:29.477
I love dulsing.

00:04:29.477 --> 00:04:31.539
Most Grand Mananers do, too.

00:04:31.539 --> 00:04:32.738
We love it.

00:04:32.738 --> 00:04:34.877
I know guys who have to keep some on hand

00:04:34.877 --> 00:04:36.437
during the winter months

00:04:36.437 --> 00:04:38.642
when you can't really buy any fresh dulse.

00:04:38.642 --> 00:04:40.204
They keep them in their deep freeze and

00:04:40.204 --> 00:04:41.369
hopefully it lasts them until

00:04:41.369 --> 00:04:44.753
the next season starts up again.

00:04:44.753 --> 00:04:46.936
You can't feed a Grand Mananer bad dulse.

00:04:46.936 --> 00:04:48.098
They won't accept it.

00:04:48.098 --> 00:04:49.664
It's gotta be good, fresh dulse,

00:04:49.664 --> 00:04:53.164
or they won't have anything to do with it.

00:04:54.816 --> 00:04:58.149
(light accordion music)

